.TH BLASTOFF 1 "2026-10-21" "blastoff 0.1.12" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
blastoff \- manage Starship themes and stored module configurations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B blastoff
.RI [ options ] " command " [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Bash and PowerShell entry points use one Python 3.11+ core.
Stored themes are independent of the active Starship configuration.
Listing never generates presets or writes files.
With no arguments, blastoff prints the same full help as -h or --help:
stdout contains help, stderr is empty, and the exit status is 0.
Global flags without a command also show help, except --json, which retains
the JSON welcome object. Invalid options and values still fail.
Root and nested help share a branded header and version.
Use the supplied 37-column, 13-row Braille logo when it fits, colored with a
left-to-right purple-to-pink gradient. Narrow, dumb, non-Unicode or redirected
output uses a compact ASCII fallback.
NO_COLOR retains fitting artwork uncolored.
Missing artwork is harmless. No logo scripts or original ANSI files execute.
Help groups commands by task and colors headings/names, with plain
descriptions.
All options, aliases and subcommand help remain available.
No discovery, config reads or subprocesses run for implicit or explicit help.
Auto color requires a terminal; NO_COLOR overrides --color always.
Color flags work before or after help, including nested help.
JSON help remains undecorated text; no-command JSON remains a single object.
No Nerd Font or image assets are required.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list, -l, --list
List local themes and built-in presets from starship preset --list.
Terminal and popup reports use muted local:/preset: prefixes, blue names,
cyan headings/counts, green current markers and yellow modified/history markers.
Ordinary redirected output retains its existing format and textual markers.
Trusted provenance marks unchanged loaded content [current] and edited content
[last loaded; modified]. Changed local sources add source changed; missing or
unsafe sources add source unavailable. Missing active content is identified as
last loaded; active configuration missing. An absent choice has an Origin line.
Unknown or untrusted tracking never guesses a theme; use blastoff current for
explicit local-content matching. Save-as retains last-loaded history.
Inspect only the record, active config and at most one local source; detected
concurrent changes suppress a current claim. Unreadable config leaves choices
available; verbose details go to stderr. JSON listing remains unchanged and
skips identity inspection. Presets are not regenerated to check current
definitions.
Local files remain available if Starship is missing or preset discovery fails.
Combined listing returns 0 even when empty; unavailable presets warn on
stderr.
JSON listing instead includes preset_discovery with status ok/unavailable and
error null/reason in its single stdout object, without a stderr warning.
Successful empty discovery is distinct from unavailable presets.
Explicit preset requests still fail with status 3 on discovery failure.
.TP
.B theme apply NAME, -t NAME, --theme NAME
Apply a local theme, otherwise a built-in preset.
Use local:NAME or preset:NAME to select an explicit source.
Validate TOML and back up the active configuration before atomic replacement.
.TP
.B theme save [NAME]
Save active content to a local theme. Explicit NAME collisions require
--force.
Without NAME, require a human terminal and use known provenance to ask:
Save edits to "<theme>"? [y/as/N].
Case-insensitive y/yes authorizes backed-up replacement without another force
flag;
a/as asks a name; n/no/Enter cancels (130); unknown input reprompts.
Save-as collisions ask Replace existing theme "<name>"? [y/N].
Missing, changed, renamed, deleted, ambiguous or unknown origins and presets
lead to save-as or cancellation, never a guessed overwrite.
Noninteractive/JSON use requires NAME. Dry-run reports a target or unresolved
name.
.TP
.B theme edit
Edit the active config via a private sibling working copy, preserving stored
themes.
Editor order: nonempty EDITOR, nvim, vim, vi, code, nano.
Support quoted executable paths and arguments without shell evaluation.
Broken explicit editors fail rather than fall back; shell/batch launchers are
refused.
Known VS Code CLIs get --wait and GUI Vim gets -f; other GUI editors need wait
args.
Back up before editing; validate UTF-8 TOML and recheck the original
fingerprint
before atomic publication. Reopen the working pathname after atomic editor
saves.
Failed, invalid or conflicted edits remain at a reported recovery path.
Active leaf links are preserved by default; the regular target is locked,
backed up and atomically updated.
Unsafe parents and library targets are refused.
Use --replace-link to convert the link instead;
its old target is left untouched.
Requires human terminal input/output, except dry-run reports target/editor
only.
.TP
.B theme copy SOURCE NAME
Copy a local theme or preset:NAME to a local theme.
.TP
.B theme import PATH NAME
Import a UTF-8 TOML file from an absolute path.
.TP
.B theme delete NAME --force
Back up and delete a stored theme. The active config remains unchanged.
Refuse deletion when the stored file is the active config path
or its symlink target.
.TP
.B preset list | preset apply NAME | preset save NAME AS
List, apply or save Starship's built-in presets.
.TP
.B module list | module save MODULE [NAME] | module load NAME
Store or replace an explicit TOML table and its descendants.
Dotted paths such as custom.clock are supported.
Loading preserves unrelated configuration values and backs up the old config.
Root dotted-key and inline-table module layouts may be refused safely.
.TP
.B module show [NAME]
Show saved TOML without loading/merging. Terminals show the name and bat/plain
text.
Redirected stdout contains original TOML bytes only; JSON has name, path and
toml.
Without NAME, browse with fzf previews: Enter shows, Escape/Ctrl-C cancels
(130).
No modules returns 1, missing fzf returns 3 with named-command guidance,
and noninteractive browsing returns 2. Ctrl-X toggles the visible preview.
Without NAME, --fancy [unicode|ascii] or --fzf select exclusive presentation
modes;
named show rejects these flags. Fancy Unicode is default.
.TP
.B module delete NAME --force
Back up and delete a stored module snippet.
Refuse deletion when the stored file is the active config path
or its symlink target.
.TP
.B backup create | backup list | backup restore ID
Snapshot, list or restore active-config backups. Listing is readable human
output
by default: ID, UTC creation time when known, kind, stat size and source.
Empty/malformed entries have explicit messages. --json requests the stable
result;
redirecting output does not switch to JSON.
Terminal and popup reports have a heading/count, separated records, blue IDs and
labels, resource-styled paths and semantic warning/error messages. Complete IDs
and values wrap at narrow widths. Color-disabled human views keep this layout.
Ordinary redirected text retains its two-space metadata and combined Kind/Size
row when it fits. The example below shows that redirected format.
Creation time is parsed as UTC from a recognized UTC ID; unknown dates stay
unknown. Sizes remain exact bytes. Source/reason and missing-file notices
remain.
Illustrative main fields (actual values and additional metadata vary):
.nf
20261013T160722Z-68908267f7dd
  Created: 2026-10-13 16:07:22 UTC
  Kind: stored | Size: 6056 B

20261014T000001Z-123456789abc
  Created: 2026-10-14 00:00:01 UTC
  Kind: config | Size: 128 B
.fi
Restore verifies the snapshot checksum and backs up the current config.
Stored-theme backups can be recovered using theme import.
For stored-module backups, inspect and validate the snapshot before copying it
to a new module file; theme import does not restore module storage.
.TP
.B backup delete ID [ID ...] | backup delete --pick | backup delete --all
Permanently delete selected config/stored backup pairs. Modes are exclusive.
Print selected IDs, kinds and count, then ask Delete N selected backups
permanently?
[y/N]. Accept case-insensitive y/yes; n/no/Enter/EOF/Escape cancels.
Tab/Shift-Tab select multiple backups in fzf. With --pick, --fancy
[unicode|ascii]
or --fzf select exclusive styles; other deletion modes reject presentation
flags.
Fancy Unicode is default; no additional bare style aliases are backup IDs.
--yes or -y bypasses confirmation.
Without a TTY or with JSON require explicit IDs/--all and --yes; dry-run needs
no
confirmation and never launches the picker. Missing fzf never expands scope.
Bare all is --all only as the sole target; trailing y/yes authorize that
selection.
After -- all tokens are literal IDs, e.g. backup delete --yes -- all y yes.
--all freezes its set before confirmation; later-created backups are excluded.
Metadata source/link paths are never deletion destinations. Refuse unsafe
links,
active-file aliases, malformed/orphan metadata and checksum mismatches before
execution. Valid metadata with an absent payload may be explicitly removed.
Delete payload before metadata; report per-ID partial failures without
rollback.
Pre-execution cancellation deletes nothing; an empty --all set is success.
.TP
.B pick [--fancy [unicode|ascii] | --fzf], pick fancy, pick fzf
Fancy Unicode is the default. Choose and apply using fzf, gum or a numbered
terminal menu. Explicit --fzf / pick fzf requires fzf (missing/failed: exit 3)
and uses compatible user styling without a Blastoff palette, height or layout.
Opposing styles are usage error 2.
Cancellation returns 130 and leaves config/storage unchanged.
Escape or Ctrl-C cancels in fzf/gum. In the line-oriented numbered menu,
use an empty line, Escape then Enter, EOF or Ctrl-C.
Basic fzf honors compatible FZF_DEFAULT_OPTS_FILE then FZF_DEFAULT_OPTS
settings.
Appearance, search and safe navigation/query-edit bindings are inherited.
Ignore executable/event bindings, auto-selection/filtering, listeners, history
writes,
walkers, field/output remapping, preview commands and unknown settings.
Verbose explains omitted options. GUM settings remain isolated.
Fancy owns purple/pink colors, reverse layout, height 90%, border and label.
Connected Unicode borders are default; explicit ascii and non-Unicode
terminal fallback are available. pick fancy aliases bare --fancy.
Ctrl-X toggles initially visible source previews in both styles.
Use a right pane at 60 percent while it has at least 60 columns, otherwise
below
at 45 percent. Fzf recalculates on resize; short terminals do not start
hidden.
Selection/cancellation/preview keybindings and color controls take precedence.
Gum and numbered fallbacks show source labels and instructions without a pane.
The shipped preview helper shows sanitized, bounded TOML, never a live prompt.
No theme commands are executed or active config changed by previewing.
Preset generation uses bounded reads/timeouts and a disposable session cache.
Only the shipped preview helper runs; safe inherited navigation bindings
remain.
Bat receives sanitized TOML on stdin with paging disabled; normal
theme/config/
cache discovery is preserved (BAT_THEME, BAT_CONFIG_PATH, BAT_CONFIG_DIR,
BAT_CACHE_PATH). No user cache rebuild or bundled theme override. Plain
fallback
works without it. Preview bounds: 64 KiB, 200 lines, 512 characters per line.
Cache at most 64 visited results of at most 256 KiB, including failed
previews.
Preview results are snapshots; application re-reads and validates the source.
Failed preset discovery warns on stderr and preserves local choices.
Empty choices return 1 without opening a picker;
invalid numbered input returns 2.
.TP
.B current
Show active path, file/link status, exact local matches and independent
last-loaded
history. Editing does not erase origin. JSON adds a provenance object to old
fields.
Use the shared Dots heading, label, path and status styles; no preset guessing.
Missing config and no local match have distinct messages.
.TP
.B doctor
Show version, Python, paths, config presence and executable availability.
Use compact labeled human output, not JSON, by default.
Missing pickers are optional; availability is not tool/config validation.
Neither status command launches tools or writes state.
Both honor NO_COLOR and redirection; doctor JSON retains its original schema.
Control characters in human path values are escaped.
.TP
.B migrate PATH
Copy valid themes from an absolute legacy directory.
Refuse collisions. Preserve source files and the active configuration.
Migration is per-file atomic; an I/O failure can leave a partial copied set.
.TP
.B completion bash|zsh|fish
Print static shell completion code to stdout. Do not edit shell profiles.
Completion is positional: local source names/prefixes, stored modules, paired
backup filenames, import paths and migration directories. Destination names
and
module-save routes remain free text.
No live preset lookup or application launch.
Backup completion does not validate metadata, kind or checksum.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -v, --verbose
Send diagnostics to stderr, as JSON records with --json. No configuration
dump.
.TP
.B --dry-run
Report intended actions and unresolved choices without directories, locks,
backups,
editor/picker sessions or tracking writes. Local validation remains active.
Preset mutation generation is deferred to avoid subprocess cache side effects.
Read-only commands retain their ordinary behavior.
.TP
.B -y, --yes
Authorize backup delete's explicit selection; not a global force substitute.
.TP
.B --force
Permit backed-up replacement of a stored file or backed-up deletion.
.TP
.B --replace-link
Replace an active config symlink with a regular file, recording its old link
and contents in a backup. Never modify the link target.
.TP
.B --json
Explicitly request undecorated JSON. Human output is otherwise the default,
including when redirected. Success goes to stdout; errors/verbose go to
stderr.
Raw completion scripts and redirected TOML retain their documented formats.
.TP
.B --color auto|always|never
For current, list/theme list, theme save, backup list and doctor reports,
explicit --color overrides DOTS_COLOR, then automatic detection applies.
Nonempty NO_COLOR suppresses automatic color; explicit always overrides it.
Help artwork and application pickers retain their existing NO_COLOR policy.
.TP
.B --version
Print the semantic version without invoking Starship.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EDITOR
Nonempty explicit editor executable and arguments; no shell expansion.
Windows uses double-quote/backslash argv rules and a native executable.
.TP
.B FZF_DEFAULT_OPTS_FILE, FZF_DEFAULT_OPTS
Reviewed options file then string; at most 64 KiB each; embedded hex-color #
is preserved. Consumed inherited FZF
settings
are removed after parsing. Safety/preview controls override conflicting
options.
.TP
.B STARSHIP_CONFIG
Absolute active config path. Defaults to ~/.config/starship.toml.
A nonempty override is honored even when its file does not yet exist.
.TP
.B BLASTOFF_HOME
Absolute storage root. Defaults to ~/.config/blastoff.
Contains themes, modules, backups and path-associated provenance state.
.TP
.B BLASTOFF_PYTHON
Python executable path or command name. Defaults to python3 in Bash.
PowerShell also supports the Windows py launcher.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Suppress automatic report colors; explicit always overrides it for reports.
.TP
.B DOTS_COLOR, DOTS_ICONS
Shared report color and independent resource-icon controls: auto, always, never.
Captured menu reports retain their human layout even with both disabled.
.SH FILES
~/.config/blastoff/themes/*.toml
.br
~/.config/blastoff/modules/*.toml
.br
~/.config/blastoff/backups/*.{toml,json}
.br
~/.config/starship.toml
.SH PROVENANCE
Version-1 records live under state/provenance, keyed by normalized active
pathname.
Keep last-loaded local/preset/unknown history separate from a local save
destination.
Store content digests and file fingerprints; distinguish history from current
match.
Apply/pick associate origins; edit/module load retain them; active save sets
the save
destination. Copy/import/preset save/migration do not reassociate the active
config.
Config backups optionally include provenance; old or foreign backups restore
unknown.
External edits retain history but report changed content. No library-wide
inference
is used to authorize a save. Current's explicit exact-match listing remains
available.
.PP
Atomically publish state after successful content writes. A pending sibling
marker
makes incomplete tracking untrusted for implicit overwrite. Reads never repair
it.
On partial failure, inspect active/stored content and backups; explicit
successful
apply/save/restore reestablishes state. Missing/corrupt/future records are
unknown.
This is not multi-file crash atomicity. Uninstall retains state and editor
recovery.
.SH SAFETY
The storage root may be a directory symlink or Windows junction.
For example, ~/.config/blastoff may link to ~/dots/config/blastoff.
Resolve that root once, including relative links and linked ancestors;
all link targets must be existing directories (at most 40 redirects).
Leave the link intact; report canonical storage paths in doctor and results.
Ordinary missing storage directories are created only by mutations.
.PP
Refuse non-regular files, unsafe names, linked storage children and leaf
files,
and symlink/reparse parents for active-config writes.
A linked .config directory does not authorize config writes through it;
use a real-path STARSHIP_CONFIG override instead.
Installer/profile protections and active-file --replace-link are unchanged.
Cooperating writers through storage aliases share the canonical directory
lock.
Locks are not automatically broken; inspect stale locks after interruption.
Files are limited to 4 MiB. Backups are retained until explicit backup delete
or manual removal.
No network, telemetry or prompt rendering.
Application commands do not edit shell startup files;
the public installer may add owned discovery hooks as described below.
.SH INSTALLATION
From reviewed sources, use:
.nf
bash install.sh --dry-run
bash install.sh
bash uninstall.sh --dry-run
bash uninstall.sh
.fi
No action word or --yes is required by these public wrappers.
Use --prefix PATH for an explicit absolute destination.
Defaults are native Termux PREFIX, other POSIX ~/.local,
and Windows LOCALAPPDATA/Programs/Blastoff (fallback ~/.local).
Plans show SYSTEM, distinguishing Termux (Android), WSL (Linux), Linux,
Windows and other hosts. Verbose diagnostics include detection evidence
and the Python executable. WSL uses Linux paths and Python.
MSYS2/Git Bash and Cygwin Bash lifecycle entry points stop with status 3
before launching Python and direct users to review install.ps1 or uninstall.ps1
with -WhatIf in native PowerShell 7+. No shell is started automatically.
Incompatible Bash/Python hosts are refused before destination planning.
Missing Python returns status 3 in both frontends.
Source checksums are mandatory even for dry-run; never automatically
refreshed.
Only after reviewing deliberate source changes, run bash scripts/build.sh.
Plans support -v/--verbose on stderr and --color auto|always|never; NO_COLOR
wins.
Dry-run writes nothing and executes no profiles or shell probes.
.PP
Public install prefers native completion and manual discovery directories.
Zsh receives canonical share/zsh/site-functions/_blastoff plus an owned copy
in
share/zsh/functions/Completion/Unix/_blastoff when the native directory
exists;
Termux's compiled fpath omits site-functions.
Custom prefixes may require minimal owned PATH/MANPATH/completion hooks.
Regular profile encoding and unrelated content are preserved.
Linked profiles/targets are never rewritten; unsafe integration is refused
with
manual setup instructions. Fish uses a conf.d drop-in.
When pwsh is available, an owned hook imports the absolute versioned manifest.
Native Windows runtime verification remains pending.
Profiles are never executed automatically; review activation instructions or
start a fresh shell. Bash/Zsh may need hash -r/rehash in an existing session.
.PP
Runtime payloads under share/blastoff/versions are immutable.
Repeated install restores missing support or refreshes unmodified supported
output; automatic downgrades are skipped.
Public install migrates verified legacy ~/.local ownership
when the target differs.
Target commit precedes resumable legacy cleanup; rerun install to finish
cleanup
before uninstalling. Modified owned files, unowned collisions and unsafe paths
block changes. Never delete ownership manifests or break locks to force
updates.
.PP
Uninstall removes verified owned files and exact owned profile blocks.
It retains user data, unrelated profile edits, empty directories and recovery
backups under the prefix's share/blastoff/profile-backups directory.
Per-file atomic writes do not provide full multi-file/power-loss transactions.
.PP
Compatibility: bash scripts/install.sh install --yes and uninstall --yes
remain
supported, as does native scripts/install.ps1 with the same action/flags.
Use --dry-run before changes. Fresh legacy installs leave integration manual
unless already owning it. See docs/INSTALLATION.md for manual activation.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 success; 1 operation failure; 2 usage error;
3 required dependency/editor/picker/preset failure;
130 cancellation or interruption.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
blastoff --list
blastoff theme apply local:purple
blastoff theme edit --dry-run
blastoff theme edit
blastoff theme save
blastoff theme save work
blastoff pick --fancy ascii
blastoff preset save catppuccin-powerline catppuccin
blastoff module save directory compact-directory
blastoff module load compact-directory
blastoff module show compact-directory
blastoff backup create
blastoff backup restore BACKUP-ID
blastoff backup delete --all --dry-run
blastoff backup delete BACKUP-ID --yes
.fi
.SH AUTHOR
njen

.SH POWERSHELL LIFECYCLE
.nf
\&.\\install.ps1 -WhatIf -Verbose
\&.\\install.ps1
\&.\\uninstall.ps1 -WhatIf
\&.\\uninstall.ps1
.fi
PowerShell 7+ only; no Bash/WSL dependency. -DryRun is equivalent to -WhatIf.
-ModuleRoot selects a Modules parent; install accepts -ProfilePath or
-NoProfile.
Default Windows location uses redirected Documents/PowerShell/Modules; Linux
uses
its CurrentUser data path. Never choose an arbitrary writable PSModulePath
entry.
A self-contained versioned module and exact CurrentUserAllHosts import are
owned
independently from the shell-prefix frontend. Profile encoding, unrelated
content
and pre-change backups are preserved. Foreign imports/modified blocks
conflict.
WhatIf lists exact paths/profile edits without creating files, locks or
backups.
Repeat installs repair missing owned files; downgrades/different immutable
bytes
are refused. Uninstall retains modified content, user data and recovery
backups.
No execution-policy changes or downloads. Printed Import-Module activation is
optional for the current session; otherwise open a new session.
Module import/static completion starts no tools and scans no storage. Dynamic
filename completion caches for two seconds and bounds work to 4096 entries/100
ms. Unix PowerShell without UnixMode uses a private filename-only Python
helper for dynamic names, with a two-second deadline; import/static
completion still launches no tools.
The function forwards arguments via a private data envelope and sets
LASTEXITCODE;
it never exits the parent shell. Linux/WSL and native Windows tools/paths stay
separate. Native platform/version/visual checks remain distinct verification
gates.
See docs/POWERSHELL.md for parity, profile/coexistence and benchmark details.
